WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.323
United Kingdom has given
approval to a trial

00:00:02.323 --> 00:00:05.750
that will expose healthy
volunteers to COVID-19.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:07.490
Infection is guaranteed

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:09.580
with a so-called challenge trial.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:10.520
Challenge trial.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:11.930
Challenge trial.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:13.700
As the pandemic progressed
over the last year,

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.410
you may have heard this term
challenge trial come up.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.610
The idea was that by
deliberately exposing people

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.130
to the virus, you could help
speed up the development

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:23.640
of a vaccine, and it
turns out a lot of people

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:24.760
were up for this.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:26.310
Everybody feels terrible right now.

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:28.150
Here's something that may help the world

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.390
be a little bit better,
so I was like, great,

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:31.870
that sounds good to me.

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:33.350
Let me sign up right now.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.780
Ultimately, challenge trials
did not end up being used

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.220
to develop the vaccines
that are now in circulation,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.420
but now, researchers in the UK are turning

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.290
to the challenge method,
saying it will allow them

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:44.870
to test the long-term
effectiveness of the vaccines

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:47.070
and learn more about the disease overall.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.470
It's restarted a controversy
in the scientific community

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.690
over the safety, efficacy
and morality of the practice.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.260
I spoke to some very
smart people on both sides

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:56.093
of the argument,

00:00:56.093 --> 00:00:58.810
in an attempt to answer a
question with big implications

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:00.075
for humanity's future.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.990
Okay, so we're here to talk
about human challenge trials,

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.710
but I guess, first let's talk
about typical vaccine trials.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.010
How do typical vaccine trials work?

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:17.040
Yeah, so once a vaccine
candidate has been studied

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.270
in animals and in the laboratory,

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:21.120
it moves into human studies,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.850
the idea is that once you're vaccinated

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.500
with either a placebo
of the actual candidate,

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.560
you're exposed in society to the pathogen,

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.190
and we want to see that
most of the infections

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:35.800
and disease occurred in
people with the placebo,

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.310
to determine that the vaccine

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:40.400
is actually protective and efficacious.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.130
So with a human challenge study,

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:47.850
we deliberately infect
humans, human volunteers,

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:51.740
and look at, essentially,
what happens to them.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:56.270
You say I've got the viral
agent, COVID, whatever it is,

00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:58.040
and I'm going to give it to people.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.090
This is Art Caplan.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:00.620
He's a researcher at NYU,

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.980
who's pretty firmly in the
pro-challenge trial column.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:07.940
Well, historically vaccines
are tested in large trials.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:12.940
Usually you're talking 30, 40,
50, 60,000 or more subjects.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.520
You could spend six, seven,
eight years trying to get data.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.800
A challenge study basically
is a way to speed up

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:22.240
the trial process.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.707
You may be able to get answers
as quickly as exposing 500,

00:02:25.707 --> 00:02:30.700
600 people, maybe over a
period of six months to a year.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.640
So are human challenge trials new

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.340
or have they been done before?

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.680
Human challenge trials are not new.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.140
Scientists have been doing
them for quite a long time,

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:44.053
decades, looking at various
diseases, malaria, cholera,

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.360
influenza are some of them.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:48.938
Walter Reed did it, as a matter of fact,

00:02:48.938 --> 00:02:51.950
with his malaria experiments in Cuba.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.710
They deliberately let mosquitoes
bite them to get malaria,

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.370
to test antidotes.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.320
Assumingly, again, they
don't want to kill you,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.650
although people have died
in challenge studies.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.557
We have a tradition in
space medicine of saying,

00:03:03.557 --> 00:03:05.987
"Gee, I wonder how long people can survive

00:03:05.987 --> 00:03:08.237
"extreme gravitational forces.

00:03:08.237 --> 00:03:11.120
"Let's put 'em in a centrifuge
until they pass out."

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.460
So there are many areas
of medicine, healthcare,

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.610
even public health,

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:18.710
where we do expose people
to risk deliberately,

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:19.913
in order to learn.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:25.860
This is the infamous trolley
problem in bioethics,

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:29.130
which is should you sacrifice one person

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.070
to save five people?

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:33.610
This is Liza Dawson,
the chief of bioethics

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:36.410
at the Walter Reed Army
Institute of Research.

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:38.300
She's not really opposed
to challenge trials,

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:39.700
but where COVID is concerned,

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:41.850
she's basically in the anti column.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:44.370
One of the issues is
that we have less ability

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:47.530
to predict what will happen
to people as a consequence

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:50.710
of COVID infection,
something like 10 to 20% of,

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:52.020
depending on the numbers,

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.580
have longterm consequences
of COVID infection.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.270
That's not to be sneezed at,

00:03:56.270 --> 00:04:00.080
and people of all ages
seem to be susceptible

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.280
to longer-term consequences of COVID.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.920
We are rolling the dice that
something terrible might happen

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:09.430
or something fairly bad
might happen to people

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:10.420
in these trials.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:12.137
Well, I do understand the critics

00:04:12.137 --> 00:04:13.770
and some of their points of view,

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:16.570
it's never easy to defend
deliberately giving someone

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:18.370
something that makes them sick.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:23.360
That violates a core norm of
medical ethics, do no harm,

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.340
but I'm not really buying
into the it causes more harm

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:28.520
to do a challenge study.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:30.680
It feels psychologically different

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:33.507
to give somebody a disease,
as opposed to saying,

00:04:33.507 --> 00:04:34.737
"Gee, I don't know how you got it.

00:04:34.737 --> 00:04:36.417
"Maybe it was on the subway

00:04:36.417 --> 00:04:38.077
"or maybe you should've worn a mask

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:40.010
"or maybe a new variant appeared."

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:43.880
We may feel psychologically,
we didn't do it, nature did it.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:46.950
Nonetheless, at the
cemetery, they don't care.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:48.840
You're relying on nature to infect you

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.800
but it's not like people aren't exposed.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:54.740
I'm not sure that that's
significantly morally better

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:57.240
than just saying, I'll
give you the disease

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:59.800
and we'll get a faster answer.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.830
And there are a lot of
volunteers who feel the same way.

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:04.460
The organization, One Day Sooner,

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:06.560
which advocates for people's
right to participate

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:07.660
in challenge trials,

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:10.650
says it's had almost
40,000 people contact them,

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:13.940
offering to take part if such
a trial were to be conducted.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:15.890
Two of those volunteers are Lena Jewler,

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:19.270
a recent graduate of Johns
Hopkins, and Gavriel Kleinwaks,

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:21.360
a PhD student in Colorado.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.540
Safe to say, they're
both in the pro column.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:26.040
So speaking personally,

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:29.670
why would you be willing to
be part of a challenge trial?

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:34.120
I think, initially, I was
looking at this as a person

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:37.440
who does human subjects research,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.690
and a person who likes to participate

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:41.810
in human challenge research,

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.350
so one of the cool things about
being a student at Hopkins

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:46.590
is that you have access to participate

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.020
in this really cool research,

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:51.910
and I've always found that
fascinating, just as a person,

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:53.190
to participate, to say, okay,

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:54.520
I'm contributing to the science

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:58.080
that may help someone
else the road, and then,

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:59.340
the challenge aspect of it,

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:02.420
I was really swayed by this argument

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:06.190
of this is so much more efficient,

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:07.580
this is so much more effective.

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:12.270
I would say the very first
place my mind went was...

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:16.890
So I'm Jewish, and in our
philosophical traditions,

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.070
it said that to save a single life,

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:21.113
it's as if you've saved an entire world.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:23.830
In the argument for challenge trials,

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:26.390
the original argument back in March,

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:29.500
it was estimated that
developing a vaccine...

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:32.210
Every day sooner that
you develop a vaccine

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:35.620
saves 10,000 lives,

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.670
and I thought that that's amazing.

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:41.550
I would do whatever it
took to develop a vaccine

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:44.100
one day sooner, which
is why the organization

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:44.933
is called that.

00:06:44.933 --> 00:06:46.680
We all know what it means
to donate your time.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:48.720
We all know what it means
to donate your money,

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.550
but this was an opportunity
I felt to donate health,

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:55.110
to try to share some of
my health with others.

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:57.220
So there are reasonable
arguments on either side

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:59.150
of the ethics debate and a
lot of people are willing

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:00.330
to take the risk,

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:02.640
but some offer a different
kind of criticism

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:03.880
of challenge trials,

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:05.700
that they wouldn't actually
accomplish their goal

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:08.400
of speeding up vaccine development.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.200
That's the big beef put
forward by Charles Weiier,

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.170
a Canadian bioethicist.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:15.820
He says the current UK
study is the perfect example

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:18.550
of just how long
challenge trials can take.

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:19.470
What they're doing,

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:21.290
so they've spent a number of months

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:23.410
developing the challenge strain,

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:25.430
so making sure they've
got a stable strain,

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:28.840
where they haven't introduced mutations

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:30.940
through the manufacturing process.

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:34.330
The second step is what
they're just embarking on now

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:36.910
to actually figuring out the protocol

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:39.750
for how they're gonna
administer this virus,

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:43.070
and so they're just
beginning that process now,

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:47.040
with incremental steps of
increasing dose of virus.

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:50.010
Each of those steps is going
to take at least a month,

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:55.010
and so we're many months away
from them being at the point

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:58.850
where they can even test the vaccine.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:01.630
The advocates have challenged
studies by and large.

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:03.920
They haven't talked about
all this preparatory work

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:05.360
that needs to be done.

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:08.250
Even from the first article
that was published about this,

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:11.960
they talked as if next week,
we could just start doing this.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:14.490
Right, you just pump a
room full of COVID-19

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:15.323
and then everybody gets it.

00:08:15.323 --> 00:08:16.620
Yeah, right, and of course,

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:21.490
these studies are much
more complicated than that.

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:23.370
And then there's the fact that
we've got the current slate

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:25.800
of COVID-19 vaccines in record time,

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.030
without using challenge trials.

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:32.930
The reason why we got a vaccine so quickly

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:36.230
was because of a decade's worth of work

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:39.600
on related coronaviruses that cause SARS

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:43.450
and that cause MERS, and
I think we had the genome

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:46.590
of SARS-CoV-2, the novel coronavirus,

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:50.780
published on January
10th, I think, of 2020.

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:55.380
Three days later, we had
our first candidate vaccine.

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:58.940
63 days later, that vaccine
was tested for the first time

00:08:58.940 --> 00:08:59.870
in human beings.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:02.280
By September of last year,

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:05.720
we had six large phase three trials

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:10.490
planning to involve 280,000 people.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:13.400
The monumental size of
the achievement here

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:15.940
would be difficult to overstate.

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:18.060
Well, that's traditional
vaccine development

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:21.050
with new technology and it
just highlights my point.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:25.540
Challenge studies are
just the wrong technology

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:28.133
for a rapidly moving pandemic.

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:32.480
But some still see challenge trials

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:35.320
playing a crucial role in
the pandemic, ironically,

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:38.450
precisely because we now
have working vaccines.

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:41.320
As soon as things start
to look very promising,

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:43.650
and we basically are in a
situation where those people

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:46.720
who are in a large scale
trial, let's just use COVID,

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:48.110
are gonna start to say, wait a minute,

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:50.150
I don't want to be in the
placebo group anymore,

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:51.240
I want to drop out,

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.440
and you can't finish the study ethically.

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:55.550
You've got to let those people know

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:56.810
you're in the placebo group.

00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:58.180
You can be pretty certain

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:00.250
that nearly everybody will drop out

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:03.360
because you're not going to
hang around in the placebo group

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:06.880
are very likely, so the studies
that we have undergoing,

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:11.030
that are underway now, of COVID
vaccines, are falling apart,

00:10:11.030 --> 00:10:13.640
because people are dropping
out of them left and right,

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:16.280
and so we're not going to get
the answers that we might want

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:18.900
to things like how long
does this vaccine last,

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:20.190
in terms of protection,

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:22.820
or do we know whether it's protecting us

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:26.310
against new variants of
the virus that appear.

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:28.380
Some also see challenge trials as a path

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:29.840
to developing new vaccines

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:31.780
that improve on the existing ones.

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:32.810
Obviously, at this point,

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:36.920
we've got three approved
vaccines in the US,

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:39.820
and maybe eight or nine total globally,

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:43.350
but there's still a lot of
benefit to these sorts of trials.

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:47.250
We can expand the total pool
of how many different types

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:49.130
of vaccines we have available,
and that's important,

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:51.190
because a lot of the vaccine
candidates we have now,

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:53.670
like the MRNA vaccines,
they're hard to transport,

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:54.740
they take two doses,

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:58.080
all of these things that if we
continue to try new vaccines,

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:00.610
we can find better vaccines
that are easier to distribute

00:11:00.610 --> 00:11:02.210
and that has a lot of equity implication.

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:03.810
We can get those to rural areas,

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:06.620
different countries with
less infrastructure.

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:09.263
So that's like the big draw here.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:14.620
Predicting the future is more of an art

00:11:14.620 --> 00:11:17.950
than a science, especially
when it comes to global crises.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:19.340
Almost no one would have guessed

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:21.970
that we'd have several working
vaccines for a coronavirus

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:23.030
within a year,

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:26.220
so it's impossible to say exactly
what role challenge trials

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:27.700
will play moving forward,

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:29.100
but if there's anything that we've learned

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:29.933
from the pandemic,

00:11:29.933 --> 00:11:32.760
it's that the question of which
path is the more ethical one

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:35.520
often doesn't have an obvious answer.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:37.330
The other thing about
challenge trials I'll say,

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:39.094
'cause I don't want to dismiss them,

00:11:39.094 --> 00:11:40.632
I'm not trying to
dismiss their importance,

00:11:40.632 --> 00:11:45.440
is if we get to a safer
way to do challenge trials,

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:49.940
then I'm all in favor of using
them, once they're safer.

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:52.030
Communities broadly
would need to understand

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:54.970
what that's about and
basically endorse it,

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:57.110
which is part of the
recommendations from the WHO

00:11:57.110 --> 00:11:59.910
and others, but if they're safer,

00:11:59.910 --> 00:12:02.500
such that we're not asking
people to take those risks,

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:05.200
I think they could be really
important tool in the toolbox,

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:08.530
and just as we do them
now in malaria trials,

